      idea: "Chapter 8 opens with a culture change, not an ad. David Ogilvy ran his agency *and* acted as its creative director. Young says most leaders who climb by handling clients and running offices can't do both, so they need a creative partner. His was **Tham Khai Meng**, a quiet Singaporean who had led Ogilvy's creatively strong Asian network for 11 years. Young recruited him over kebabs in Lahore, ignoring the conventional advice to hire a big-name star, and then shared a single desk with him in New York, an alien idea there, so nobody could ever play the business side against the creative side.\n\nIn 2008 they inherited a middling record at Cannes: Ogilvy sat in the third division because award-winning work hadn't been a priority for years. Khai told Young he couldn't work somewhere that settled for slightly above average. So they set a goal that sounded crazy: become Cannes **Network of the Year** within five years. They ran it like a military mission crossed with a political campaign under one cry, **“Give me Gold”**, and got there in two. A key mechanism was the **Cadre**: once a year the best creatives meet (in places like Cuzco and the Maasai Mara) to review and raise the work, and an office earns its seat only through award points.\n\nYoung admits awards are dangerous: they reward only what a jury can recognize, and Ogilvy himself was wary of them. But he gives **three reasons** they now matter:\n1. **Clients came to Cannes** (P&G first, then everyone), turning it from self-indulgence into a forum on creativity's business value, with effectiveness and technology categories.\n2. **Talent economics:** as in pro football, success depends on owning a disproportionate share of the best people, and the best want to work where winning is core, not a luxury.\n3. **Holding companies and analysts** began reading award tables as a sign of strength, just as bloggers were mocking “Big Dumb Agencies”.\n\nFor Ogilvy's 100th birthday in 2011 they rolled a red carpet down the whole Croisette, then mailed it back to clients as insoles (see the example). They came second that year, won in 2012, and four more times after.\n\nThe deeper lesson: the real enemy was their own **size**. A big firm stays sharp only if it behaves as if every prize is life or death. And they never chased Cannes alone: it was always paired with the **Effies**, which measure effectiveness. The summit is winning both, which Dove *Evolution* did first, in 2007 (its own Hall of Fame case earlier in the book).",
