      idea: "Young's Hall of Fame case starts with his own first aftershave. Launched in 1938, Old Spice had become grandpa's brand: a profitable half-billion-dollar business, but musty and the definition of uncool. Then Unilever's **Axe** arrived in the US, selling the oldest weapon there is (use it and women find you more attractive), and Old Spice looked older still.\n\n**1. The strategy.** By 2006 the brand needed new thinking and a new agency. Wieden+Kennedy Portland spent two days in the archives and reframed it from **“old” to “experienced”**: not your grandfather, your elder brother; “experience is everything”, aimed at teens and twenty-somethings. The launch spots, with Bruce Campbell in a wood-panelled library, were the right direction but, Young says, feel laboured now: irreverent, without the style or the buyer insight that came next.\n\n**2. The threat.** In 2009 Unilever was about to launch **Dove Men+Care**, and Young watched from the other side: Ogilvy was building a ‘comfortable in your own skin’ positioning, a microsite and a Super Bowl film.\n\n**3. The client who let go.** P&G had folded Gillette into a new male grooming unit, and one of its leaders, Rishi Dhingra, briefed the agencies with guardrails plus freedom: very un-P&G. The team embraced phrases like “ridiculously masculine” and refused predictable answers. W+K even insisted on a **“pre-nuptial”**: no pretest, breaking P&G's rule.\n\n**4. The idea.** Isaiah Mustafa, an ex-NFL player and small-time actor, became “The man your man could smell like”, beautifully shot by Tom Kuntz. Part of the magic is his attractive rebelliousness; part is strategy: **about 60% of men's body wash is bought by women**, so he talks to them, which sparks a real conversation between women and men.\n\n**5. Going social.** In July 2010 Mustafa filmed the **Response Campaign**: 186 personalized video replies to fans on Facebook, Twitter and YouTube in two and a half days. Six million views in 24 hours and 76% of the online buzz.\n\n**The upshot:** Dove Men+Care still established itself, but Old Spice transformed its brand equity, returned to volume growth and expanded globally. Two years later in India the pretests were back, and the ad scored top anyway, probably because young Indian men had already seen it online.\n\nYoung closes with the late Tim Broadbent's warning: we want something first and invent the rationale afterwards, and conventional research confuses the two. We learn about brands without noticing we're learning, so a pretest that only checks whether people remember a selling point or a key image misses how brands actually work.",
