      idea: "Bigness has a second cause: the interplay between **idea and execution**. Young's illustration: hand a weak idea to a master director like Alan Parker and it will come out well; hand a strong idea to a hack and it will come out poorly. So he has always trained his account people with a simple **2×2 matrix**: idea strength on one axis, execution quality on the other. Only strong idea plus strong execution deserves the trophy. Try it on this year's Super Bowl ads: despite paying millions for 30 seconds, many land in the wrong box, because slick production and a celebrity can't make up for a missing idea. He calls the matrix a lethally effective career builder.\n\nExecution also fails through **inconsistency**. Volvo Trucks' *The Epic Split* (Forsman & Bodenfors, 2013, a Cannes Grand Prix) is a strong idea: an unexpected combination of *stability* and *movement*, with Jean-Claude Van Damme doing the splits between two reversing trucks to prove the precision of Volvo Dynamic Steering. Over 85 million people watched. It pulls the same lever as the famous 1980s Krazy Glue demonstration: both defy expectations. But people forget it was one film in a bigger campaign. The others (a hamster steering a truck, Volvo's CEO hanging from a truck's hook, a technician buried as a truck rolls over him) faded, not for lack of a celebrity but because they weren't expressing the same idea. They were stunts, not surprises.\n\nThen Young names **two new jobs** ideas have in the digital age:\n1. **A system of management.** Amid the chaos, the idea is the frame of reference for everything the brand does: a principled compass to accept or reject activities, and the visual and physical home for every interaction with customers. An editorial role.\n2. **A connector** to other disciplines that are also being redefined. Brian Collins, who ran Ogilvy's Brand Integration Group, calls *brand* the promise and *design* the performance: evidence of a promise kept. Brands are something to build *from*, not *to*. For Collins brands live in four places (culture, environment, products and the mind), so systems designers and engineers sit alongside the storytellers. Asked by Hershey's for a Times Square billboard, they delivered a chocolate-factory tourist destination instead, which became the highest-grossing retail space in New York.\n\nWhy it matters: clients feel fragmented. General Mills' CMO Mark Addicks said marketers live in chaos and are desperate for a rulebook; they can't get a single view of their customer. Digital is biased toward clever small tactics that bubble up and add up to nothing. The cure, he says, is simple: find one Big Idea of your own and exploit it relentlessly.",
